# Compiled MetaTrader programs and archives are binary. Say so.
#
# Without this, git decides by sniffing the first 8000 bytes for a NUL and
# guessing. `core.autocrlf` is true on the machine this is developed on, so
# a compiled EA that happened to look like text would have its line endings
# rewritten on checkout and be silently corrupted -- a .ex4 that no longer
# loads, with nothing in the diff to show why, because git would still
# report the file as unchanged.
#
# The heuristic has held so far (git reports these as `Bin` in diffs today),
# which is exactly the problem: it is luck rather than a rule, and the next
# EA to be compiled is a fresh roll. These are the files that reach a live
# MT4/MT5 terminal and place real orders, so the guess is not worth keeping.
*.ex4  binary
*.ex5  binary
*.zip  binary

# Deliberately NOT declaring text handling for anything else.
#
# Adding `* text=auto` or an eol rule for the .mq4/.mq5 sources would make
# git renormalise files it has already stored, which shows up as every line
# of every affected file being modified -- a diff nobody can review, mixed
# into whatever change happens to be in flight. Nothing here is broken, so
# there is nothing to fix. This file exists to protect the binaries.
